cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this apparatus, please cite it as below. When citing a passage,
  cite the printed original — the paragraph grids exist so that you can;
  where a numbering is this site's own (stated per module), name the site
  as the source of the numbering.
title: "Calculemus: Philosophical Predecessors of AI"
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: Fassbender
    given-names: Pantaleon
abstract: >-
  A digital research apparatus for the philosophical prehistory of the AI
  debate: twenty-five public-domain editions in four lines — the logic line
  (Llull, Kircher, Hobbes, Leibniz, Boole, Frege, with its world roots in
  al-Khwārizmī, Ibn Khaldūn's zāʾirja and the Yijing), the machine line
  (Pascal, Lovelace, Jevons, Peirce), the counter-voices (Avicenna,
  Descartes, La Mettrie, Poe, Butler, Kapp) and the narrative line of the
  animated word (Homer/Aristotle, Liezi, the golem, the brazen head, Goethe,
  Čapek) — with paragraph-exact citation, a cross-corpus concordance, a term
  atlas, curated reading paths and a citation-bound dialogue. The corpus
  ends, deliberately, at the threshold of Turing.
keywords:
  - history of artificial intelligence
  - digital humanities
  - philosophy of mind
  - combinatorics
  - public domain editions
  - Leibniz
  - computational theory of mind
license:
